    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly! System friction in the context of traffic flow can be thought of as the resistance that slows down the movement of vehicles, much like how a group of stubborn, mischievous goblins might occasionally throw a wrench into a well-oiled machine. In signalized intersections, this friction often comes from the stop-and-go nature of the traffic lights. Even when the lights are green, drivers might hesitate, causing a ripple effect that slows down the overall flow. This is akin to a goblin throwing a pebble into a pond, creating ripples that spread out and disrupt the smooth surface.\n\nIn roundabouts, the system friction is generally lower because vehicles can move at a more constant speed, reducing the chances of sudden stops. However, unexpected spikes in delay can still occur, much like a sudden goblin ambush in an otherwise peaceful forest. These spikes can be caused by a variety of factors, such as:\n\n1. **Driver Behavior**: Just as goblins might suddenly appear from the shadows, drivers can also behave unpredictably. A driver might suddenly brake or change lanes without signaling, causing a ripple effect that disrupts the flow.\n\n2. **Incidents**: Accidents or breakdowns can cause sudden congestion, much like a",
